.TH PATCH 1
.SH NAME
patch \- apply unified diffs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B patch
[
.B -nRz
]
[
.B -p
.I n
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Patch
reads unified diffs, as
.B diff -u
and
.I git
write them, from the
.I files
or its standard input, and applies them to the files they name.
It checks every diff before it writes any file:
if one does not apply,
.I patch
says why and changes nothing.
It prints the name of each file it changes, one a line, and both names of a renamed file.
.PP
.I Patch
ignores the text before, between and after the diffs, such as the headers of a mail.
A diff begins with a line
.BI "--- " old
and a line
.BI "+++ " new ,
or with
.IR git 's
line
.BI "diff --git " "old new"
and the lines of its header.
A name ends at a tab, and a name in double quotes has C's escapes.
.B /dev/null
as the old name makes the new file, and as the new name removes the old one.
Every other name must be local, as
.IR islocal (2)
says: not absolute, not beginning with
.BR # ,
and not leading out of the directory once cleaned.
.I Patch
cleans each name, so
.B ./f
and
.B f
are one file.
Of
.IR git 's
header,
.I patch
takes
.BR "new file mode" ,
.BR "deleted file mode" ,
.BR "old mode" ,
.BR "new mode" ,
.B rename from
and
.BR "rename to" ,
and
.B copy from
and
.BR "copy to" ,
and skips the
.B index
and
.B similarity
lines.
A diff with a header and no hunks still makes, removes, renames or copies its file:
an empty file, a rename that changes nothing inside, a change of mode.
A mode of
.B 100755
gives the file execute permission wherever it has read permission, and
.B 100644
takes execute permission away.
.I Patch
refuses a binary patch, and the modes of symbolic links and submodules.
.PP
Without
.IR git 's
header,
.I patch
changes the old file in place, or the new one when there is no old one.
A rename removes the old file.
.PP
A hunk applies where its old lines, its context and the lines it removes, are in the file exactly.
.I Patch
looks first at the line the hunk names, moved by as many lines as the hunk before it moved,
and then one line further each way in turn, below before above, through the file.
A hunk that names the first line must apply there, and one with no context after its
changes must end at the end of the file, as
.IR git 's
apply holds them; so a diff made with no context, as
.B diff -U0
makes it, needs
.BR -z .
The hunks of a file apply in their order, each after the one before.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.BI -p " n"
Remove from each name the shortest prefix that holds
.I n
slashes.
By default
.I n
is 1 for a diff with
.IR git 's
header, whose names begin
.B a/
and
.BR b/ ,
and 0 for one without, since
.IR diff (1)
gives the names as typed:
.B "diff -u x x.new | patch"
changes
.BR x .
The names of
.IR git 's
rename and copy lines have no such prefix, and lose one component fewer.
.TP
.B -n
Check the diffs as
.I patch
would apply them and print the files they would change, but write nothing.
.TP
.B -R
Apply the diffs in reverse, from the new files to the old.
.TP
.B -z
Take the diffs as made with no context, as
.IR git 's
apply
.B --unidiff-zero
does: a hunk is held only when it adds lines before the first, and the rest go by their numbers.
.PP
.I Patch
writes each file's new text to a temporary file beside it,
.IB file .patch pid\f1.
When it has written them all, it moves each old file aside, to
.IB file .patchold pid\f1,
puts the temporary files in their places, and only then removes the old files.
If any step fails, it puts back what it moved and removes the temporary files and the directories it made,
so the files are as they were;
if it cannot put a file back, it says so, names the file that holds the old text, and exits with an error.
A file and a directory of the same name may change places:
.I patch
moves the file aside before it makes the directory, and moves the directory aside, to
.IB dir .patchold pid\f1,
before it puts the file in its place, removing the old tree only when all is done.
.I Patch
refuses a patch that makes a file under a name the patch makes a file.
If
.I patch
is killed while it commits, or cannot put files back, the old text of a file is in
.IB file .patchold pid
beside it, and its new text may be in
.IB file .patch pid\f1;
to put a file back, remove any new
.I file
and rename
.IB file .patchold pid
to
.IR file ,
and remove the
.IB file .patch pid
files.
It holds an interrupt off while it does this, and acts on it after, and it prints the names when it has done.
It makes the directories a new file needs, and removes those that a removed file leaves empty.
.SH EXAMPLES
Turn a copy of the tree
.B old
into
.BR new :
.IP
.EX
diff -ru old new >/tmp/p
cd copy
patch -p1 </tmp/p
.EE
.PP
Apply a commit that
.IR git (1)
exported, and take it back:
.IP
.EX
patch <0001-fix.patch
patch -R <0001-fix.patch
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/patch.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR diff (1),
.IR git (1),
.IR patch (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
An error names the patch, the line of the diff's header, the file and the cause,
such as the line of a hunk that does not apply.
.SH BUGS
Lines after a hunk's counts run out are text between diffs, so a hunk that miscounts its lines
loses the rest without a word.
.PP
.B -R
refuses a copy.
